    "report": "Phone numbers written with hyphens are now recognised as contact details. The change is committed on `main` as `2c5b51c` and not pushed.\n\n**What changed**\n- The phone pattern is now `\\+?\\d(?:[ .-]?\\d){8,}` in both places that hold it: Messaging's `ContactDetails.ts` (holds back in messages) and Reviews' `ReviewRules.ts` (refuses in comments and replies with `CONTACT_DETAILS`). ADR 0063 says each context keeps its own copy, so I changed both and did not share one.\n- `06-12-34-56-78` and `+33-6-12-34-56-78` are now held back or refused. `2026-12-01` is still not a phone number, since it has only eight digits. Emails, IBANs, links and the rest are untouched.\n- I added hyphen cases to both specs. I also added a check that a double hyphen or an 8-digit hyphenated number is left alone.\n\n**Verification**\n- Unit tests: 155 suites, 1053 tests pass.\n- E2E tests for conversations and reviews: 6 suites, 32 tests pass.\n- Typecheck, lint and prettier are clean on the changed files.\n- I did not start a server, so port 4471 was not used.\n\n**Choices and unclear points**\n- **Committing on `main`:** you asked for the current branch, and that is `main`. The git guidance says to branch first when on the default branch, but your instruction was explicit, so I committed there. If you want a branch, the commit can be moved.\n- **Where the rule is stated:** I updated the code comment in `ReviewRules.ts`. I left the ADRs alone, since they describe the phone rule only by reference.\n- **Hyphenated digit runs:** any run of nine or more digits joined by single separators now counts, so a reference such as `1234-5678-9` is also held back or refused. The spec allows this, and I left it as written.",
